nodecode-gemini-live is a rewrite of skappafrost's skappafrost/hermes-gemini-live:

  https://github.com/skappafrost/hermes-gemini-live
  commit cf549e7f8af858568936ccb434c7e1104eb200e9
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Ha Trung (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the Gemini Live wire (the setup frame with its model, voice,
spoken-brevity system instruction and function declarations; 16 kHz PCM up, 24 kHz
PCM down; the transcription and tool-call frames), the three delegate verbs
hermes_task, hermes_tasks and hermes_task_update with their WORK_STARTED receipt,
the task board they read, the five-voice whitelist, the spoken-brevity and
result-shaping prompts, and the rule that the model may deny, steer or stop a task
but never approve one. The plugin's browser relay, its key-redacting error path and
its "the key never reaches the renderer" split became a single in-process turn with
every error string redacted, since a Lisp cell has no renderer to protect.

What was left out, and why: the always-on duplex call (microphone capture, mute,
speaker echo cancellation and the composer control) needs a browser, which a cell
does not have, so a call here is one turn at a time, from text or a recorded WAV.
The approval button went with it: a delegated task is answered with the
WORK_STARTED receipt and named back to the calling agent, which runs it, instead
of being run inside the call and read aloud when it lands. The websocket relay
threads that let several tasks run while the user keeps talking are therefore
unnecessary and absent.
